    {
      "id": "wTkM0xyIEwo_vWD_BsLjC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3949.0140078798986,
      "y": -5701.899685313687,
      "width": 25294.353515625,
      "height": 805,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#099268",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1750224533,
      "version": 470,
      "versionNonce": 2046742009,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785137720560,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The problem is simple we are given two sorted arrays in ascending order. where the first array has m+n element out of which m element are the ones to be merged\nwith the n other elements form the other array and we are to not return anything but mutate the first array by assigning it the merged array.",
      "fontSize": 322,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The problem is simple we are given two sorted arrays in ascending order. where the first array has m+n element out of which m element are the ones to be merged\nwith the n other elements form the other array and we are to not return anything but mutate the first array by assigning it the merged array.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "qSlpAN8Y9HaVqwkZqHvrf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3953.737102866593,
      "y": -4567.388621447388,
      "width": 25621.125,
      "height": 805,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#099268",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 725821013,
      "version": 372,
      "versionNonce": 2019594039,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785137722494,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Now i was thinking i had done this kind of problem before in college with pointers so i will use that. that approach takes like O(m+n) extra space and solves it in \nm+n times as well so its  a good approach in my opiminion so i will implement it instead of any nested loop approach.",
      "fontSize": 322,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Now i was thinking i had done this kind of problem before in college with pointers so i will use that. that approach takes like O(m+n) extra space and solves it in \nm+n times as well so its  a good approach in my opiminion so i will implement it instead of any nested loop approach.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "NDHyuxMkO3thm2VdzSGvR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3909.2601558796123,
      "y": -3578.6009050003595,
      "width": 44971.4375,
      "height": 1610,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#099268",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3c",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2099092725,
      "version": 1179,
      "versionNonce": 1227457689,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785137723561,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. well i'll be damned!! i did think of an approach to overwrite those useless zeros in nums1 array. so this new approach that i got to know from gemini uses the same kind of sturcture but no extra space so we start from the back so we don't overwrite or have extra copy loops.\nso we have 3 pointers one on each array's end and one on the first array's literal end including those reamaining zeros.\nThen we just using the second array(this is crutial to use this one) until this array's empy we check if we've gone below the first index of the second array and check which one's element is bigger. then assisng that to the array at the absolute back of the first array and keep doing so\ndecreasing the pointer each time so it doesn't catch up or overwrite anything important becuase anything that'll be ever written will be on the elements that are already processed so its safe. i implemented this and this was quite fun to implement phew!",
      "fontSize": 322,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. well i'll be damned!! i did think of an approach to overwrite those useless zeros in nums1 array. so this new approach that i got to know from gemini uses the same kind of sturcture but no extra space so we start from the back so we don't overwrite or have extra copy loops.\nso we have 3 pointers one on each array's end and one on the first array's literal end including those reamaining zeros.\nThen we just using the second array(this is crutial to use this one) until this array's empy we check if we've gone below the first index of the second array and check which one's element is bigger. then assisng that to the array at the absolute back of the first array and keep doing so\ndecreasing the pointer each time so it doesn't catch up or overwrite anything important becuase anything that'll be ever written will be on the elements that are already processed so its safe. i implemented this and this was quite fun to implement phew!",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
